  ],
  "text": "Question six. How do you handle late-arriving facts? Load them at their own event time. Say Acme's 2022 order only reaches the warehouse today. Look up the customer's current row, and the order gets ten oh two: the West, a region Acme hadn't moved to yet. Look up the version valid on the order date, and it gets ten oh one, the East. And make sure it's picked up at all. A load that only takes recent order dates skips it, so select new rows by when they arrived, not when they happened. Then rebuild any periodic snapshot for the period the order lands in. An accumulating snapshot just updates its row in place.\n\nQuestion seven. How do you handle a slowly changing measure in a fact table? A measure is fixed when the event happens. This order was booked at nine hundred dollars in March. In April, it turns out it was eight hundred. Don't update it in place. Insert a reversing row, minus nine hundred, and the corrected eight hundred. Today's total is eight hundred. And March's report, run again, still says nine hundred, as it did when it was published. Overwrite the row instead, and last month's number changes under everyone who quoted it. When the rules change, like revenue recognized a new way, version the fact, or keep the restated amount in a column of its own.",
